\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
New Staging Tool
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Morten Ervik, IARC, Lyon and Giulio Napolitano, NICR, Belfast
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Possibilities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Freeze the Access version, standardize TNM in CanReg
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freeze the Access version after adding the last couple of things (copy to
 clipboard, move buttons, standardize parts).
 Provide the tool to eligible registries.
 (This could potentially be served to them via the CIN 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

portal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.) Like this CanReg users can start using it and copy paste info into TNM
 field.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Web service 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The best way to do this in the future would be to create a web service available
 to users with a valid API key.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Benefits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First of all this would make this tool availble across languages and platforms.
 Users can build calls to the web service into their own tool.
 (Like we'll do with CanReg.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, this would protect the copy rigthed tables since we don't need to
 distribute them to the users and it allow us to control access - only allowing
 those with valid keys.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Where could this be hosted?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is fairly easy to set up and run and shouldn't pose any problems, so
 it can be done here at IARC or at Queens, Belfast - or even both places.
 (In worst case scenario one could rent server space on sites like Heroku
 ( http://www.heroku.com/ ) or similar.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Technical details
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tables based 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Everything is based on tables.
 For each version of TNM (Version 6, and 7, for example - we could even
 work with patch levels, if we wanted) you have three sets of tables; Tables
 that contain the logic, tables that contain the meta data and tables that
 contain information on the coding scheme.
 These tables should be easy to read and maintain for non-programmers and
 it allows us to easily switch between versions, if needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Tables contain the logic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Four tables per site (T, N, M, GS):
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Named <Site>_T, <Site>_N, <Site>_M, i.e: 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Breast_T
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One column per variable involved in the computation
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Example, Breast_T: "Size","ChestInvasion","Ulceration","Inflammation"
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
All permutations per site, with valid/invalid results:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Example, Breast_T: "ANY","T","F","F","T4a",
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Standardize names/codes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Some additional columns for all tables: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
'Result'
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
'Message' (for error messages, warnings etc.)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Truncate tables by using 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ANY
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as keyword for overridden values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Meta-files tables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One file per TNM part and Group Stage per site (T_Prostate, N_Prostate,
 T_Breast, GS_Breast, etc...)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
One line per variable
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
'Field' (Variable name in table)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
'DisplayText'
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
'DataType' (Number, Dictionary, Boolean)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
'Group' (This can be numeric - or the name of the Group, if it is a name
 it will be dispayed as header for this group of variables.)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Coding scheme/Range will be picked up based on standard file names, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Breast_T_Size_Range
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and associated with the meta-data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the future:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
On a variable level - show up/hide when conditions are met?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
This meta data is what we'll send to clients/web interface as JSON objects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dictionaries layout
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A simple two column file containing the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Key
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the corresponding 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

DisplayText
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Ranges file layout
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A simple three column file containing the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Key
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the corresponding 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Min
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Max
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The steps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Make the tables - Pilot Breast
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Giulio will write/generate the tables based on his Access database.
 We'll pilot Breast first, as that is of intermediate complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Status:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Giulio has generated the tables needed for the T part of Breast.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Write a Ruby scripts to do the logic based on tables, receive JSON - give
 back TNM/Stage Group
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Morten will write scripts to read the tables and perform needed operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Status:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A prototype of this has been done to test the feasibility of our design
 for the tables.
 This seem to work well, so now we'll need to test them on more complex
 tables from Giulio.
 The scripts will dynamically add those as they are being created/updated
 - (in theory) without the need for further programming intervention...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Generate interface specifications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Giulio will search for available tools.
 (Possibly cross platform: Rails (/html), Java, Qt ...)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Status:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not advanced yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Write web service in Ruby 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The web service can be implemented in for example Rails, Sinatra or Padrino
 - depending on the complexity we wish.
 Morten will do this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Status:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A prototype has been done as a proof of concept.
 This is up and running and seems to work well.
 Users can get availble sites by calling get on '/sites.json', they can get
 meta data needed (including dictionaries and ranges) by calling get '/meta/<sit
e>' (where '<site>' is for example 'breast') and by calling post with relevant
 information on '/tnm/<site>' they can calculate TNM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
API keys restrictions have not yet been implemented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Write web interface in Ruby 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The web interface can be implemented in Rails, Sinatra, Padrino - depending
 on complexity of the previous steps.
 Morten will do this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Status:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A command line interface has been made as a proof of concept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Future
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some potential future developments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Show/hide variables during data entry 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Depending on rules in tables we can show/hide some fields during data entry
 to facilitate the work of users.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Batch processing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Download only the interface, enter data (CanReg: Link to Patient ID/Tumour
 Sequence), calculation when online.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Interface in CanReg
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Staging Tool could be accessed directly from CanReg if we add tools
 to load up dynamic forms based on what the server need for each site.
 We'll also need to allow the users to enter (and potentially store) their
 API key.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By bringing the tables out from a mix of tables and if-statements in Access
 and into tables we're gaining four key things.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Readability - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

anyone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 can read, and make sense of, the tables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Maintainability - since the tables have been out of the logic of the program,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

anyone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 with knowledge in the field can update them, and thus update the rules
 in the program.
 We can even maintain several different versions of TNM in parallell, if
 we require.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Portability - the same tables can be used by many tools written in any language.
 Just one set of tables needs to be maintained to serve both as a web service
 or stand alone tool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compatibility - this is fairly close to the point above, but important in
 its own.
 Users are no longer 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

forced
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to use Access (although they can, if they want - if we backport the tables
 to Access).
\end_layout

